hadiths[7]{hadithnumber,text}:
"19","On the adhan. Abbad ibn Musa narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was concerned about the prayer, how to gather the people for it. So Abdullah ibn Zayd went away, concerned about the affair of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a visitor came to him in a dream and said to him: Tell the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to command a man, when the prayer time comes, to call the adhan, saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest - mentioning the adhan in pairs - and when he finishes, let him wait until the sleeper awakes and whoever wishes to perform ablution does so. When the people gather for their prayer, let him repeat, saying the same, until when he reaches: Come to prayer, come to success, let him say: The prayer has begun, the prayer has begun. Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; there is no god but Allah - and he narrated the hadith in full."
"20","Ahmad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj; Ata informed me that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions consulted about how to appoint something by which they would gather for prayer when they wanted to; they considered the bell. While Umar ibn al-Khattab was about to buy two pieces of wood for a bell, Umar saw in a dream: Do not use the bell; rather, call the adhan for prayer. So Umar went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to tell him what he had seen, but revelation had already come with that; Umar was only surprised by Bilal calling the adhan. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Revelation has preceded you in that, when Umar told him of it."
"21","Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us; Ata al-Khurasani narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Abdullah ibn Zayd and supplicated good for him, then left him, entered the mosque and said to his Companions: Advise us of something by which the people of the mosque may be summoned - and he narrated the hadith - and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Bilal, ascend, and he said to Abdullah ibn Zayd: Stand beside him and teach him the adhan."
"22","Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us - (h) - and Makhlad ibn Khalid narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab; Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sa'd al-Mu'adhdhin informed me that Bilal came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the dawn prayer and was told: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is asleep. So Bilal said: Prayer - Makhlad said in his narration: in his loudest voice - prayer is better than sleep, and this was retained in the adhan of the Fajr prayer. And he said, from Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sa'd: My family narrated to me that Bilal..."
"23","Ziyad ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the year of the Conquest, ordered Bilal, who then called the adhan atop the Ka'bah."
"24","Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Mu'tamir - a shaykh of al-Hirah named Yazid ibn Tahman - from Ibn Sirin, that Bilal would place his fingers in his ears during part of his adhan or his iqamah, in a voice neither loud nor low. Abu Dawud said: His origin is Basran."
"25","Ahmad ibn Abi al-Hawari narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us, from Abu Amr and others, from Ibn Harmalah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one leaves the mosque after the call except a hypocrite, except one whom some need takes out while he intends to return."
